  "title": "Supplementary Table S29. Inverse-probability weight distributions, effective sample sizes and covariate balance.",
  "panel": "",
  "panel_title": "",
  "notes_before": [],
  "notes_after": [
    "Note. Joint-exposure inverse-probability weights (winsorised at the 1st/99th percentiles) for the four-group models (N = 6,471 observations). ESS: effective sample size, (Σw)²/Σw²; the smallest joint group is high EVI/high O₃. Balance: after weighting, 9 of 17 covariates (1 km) and 10 of 17 (500 m) retained maximum pairwise standardised mean differences above 0.1 across the four groups; climatic principal components remained strongly imbalanced (weighted standardised mean difference up to 0.95 at 1 km and 1.01 at 500 m). These diagnostics motivate reporting unweighted models as primary. The same joint-exposure weights were reused in the continuous-exposure sensitivity models (Table S24; 5,914 observations); the effective-sample-size percentages above are relative to the 6,471-observation joint-model frame. Abbreviations: CV, coefficient of variation; ESS, effective sample size; EVI, enhanced vegetation index; O₃, ozone."
  ],
